%[text] %[text:anchor:T_0CC94808] # Operational Amplifiers
%[text] [⇦ DC Circuit Analysis Main Menu](file:../../MainMenu.m)
%[text] **Learning Goals**
%[text] - Describe the open-loop behavior of an op amp.
%[text] - Compare ideal and non-ideal op amp behavior.
%[text] - Analyze negative feedback op amp circuits.
%[text] - Design circuits using combinations of basic op amp circuits. \
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D33645E0] ## Introduction
%[text] Operational amplifiers (op amps) are versatile high gain voltage amplifiers that originated in electronic analog computers. They are widely employed as fundamental components of analog circuits. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![opamp\_labels.png](text:image:0b5d)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Op amp symbol*
%[text] Op amps have three essential connections:
%[text] - $V\_+${"altText":"V\_+"}: non-inverting input
%[text] - $V\_-${"altText":"V\_-"}: inverting input
%[text] - $V\_{\\text{out}}${"altText":"V\_{\text{out}}"}: output  \
%[text] The op amp requires a power supply to function. The supply voltages $V\_{\\text{s}-}${"altText":"V\_{\text{s}-}"} and $V\_{\\text{s}+}${"altText":"V\_{\text{s}+}"} typically have the same value and opposite sign (for example, $\\pm 15 \\ \\text{V}${"altText":"\pm 15 \ \text{V}"}). 
%[text] The circuits used to realize op amps are quite complicated. The analysis of the internal circuitry of the op amp is challenging and is not generally performed. Instead, simplified equations are used to model the behavior of the op amp under typical conditions. This simplified approach is used in this lesson.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_30512E0A] ## Open-loop behavior
%[text] When an op amp is used in a circuit *without* a connection between its output and inputs, it is in an *open-loop* configuration. The term "open-loop" differentiates the configuration from a circuit with a feedback loop (also known as a closed-loop). 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![openloop.png](text:image:4807)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Op-amp in an open-loop configuration*
%[text] The open-loop op amp displays a simple behavior: it greatly amplifies the voltage difference between the input leads. Mathematically, this is expressed as a proportionality with a gain $A\_\\text{O}${"altText":"A\_\text{O}"} :
%[text]{"align":"center"} $V\_{\\text{out}} = A\_\\text{O}\\cdot(V\_+ - V\_-)${"altText":"V\_{\text{out}} = A\_\text{O}\cdot(V\_+ - V\_-)"}
%[text] The open-loop gain $A\_\\text{O}${"altText":"A\_\text{O}"} is typically quite large in a real op-amp (on the order of$10^5${"altText":"10^5"}). This equation is not valid in all cases. For example, in a real op amp, the output voltage will saturate near the supply voltage.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![saturated\_voltage.png](text:image:3781)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *The response of a non-ideal op amp (with* $V\_- = 0${"altText":"V\_- = 0"})*. For low voltages, the op amp responds linearly following* $V\_{\\text{out}} = A\_\\text{O}\\cdot (V\_+ - V\_-)${"altText":"V\_{\text{out}} = A\_\text{O}\cdot (V\_+ - V\_-)"}*. For large voltages, the response saturates to the supply voltage,* $V\_{\\text{out}} = V\_{\\text{s}\\pm}${"altText":"V\_{\text{out}} = V\_{\text{s}\pm}"}.
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:2bc8) **Activity.** The behavior of a non-ideal op amp is simulated in:
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![](text:image:1d22)](<matlab: OpAmpOpenLoop>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [`OpAmpOpenLoop.slx`](<matlab: OpAmpOpenLoop>)
%[text] Open the model and follow the steps listed in the canvas to complete the activity.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_11D98536] ## Feedback
%[text] For the most part, op amps are not used in an open-loop configuration. They are more commonly used in a *feedback* configuration. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![closed\_loop.png](text:image:9808)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Example of an op amp with feedback. This circuit is known as a non-inverting amplifier.*
%[text] In this configuration, the op amp output is "fed back" into one of the inputs. Normally, the output is fed back into the negative terminal. This is known as negative feedback. Generally speaking, it limits the overall amplification of the output. Variations on the circuitry used in the feedback network can produce a wide range of behaviors.
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:6e37) **Activity.** The open-loop and feedback configurations of the op amp are compared in 
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![feedbackcircuit.png](text:image:16ed)](<matlab: OpAmpFeedback>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [`OpAmpFeedback.slx`](<matlab: OpAmpFeedback>)
%[text] Open the model and follow the steps listed in the canvas to complete the activity.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A1E86B0E] ### Ideal op amp
%[text] When used in a feedback loop, it is typically satisfactory to treat the op amp with an idealized model. In the ideal model:
%[text] - The op amp is assumed to have infinite open-loop gain: $A\_\\text{O} = \\infty${"altText":"A\_\text{O} = \infty"}. 
%[text] - The power supply saturation limits are ignored (equivalent to the supply being infinite).
%[text] - The internal resistance of the op amp is ignored. \
%[text] This model is commonly used because it is valid in the typical operating range of an op amp with negative feedback.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6A3A92AF] ### Analysis of a non-inverting amplifier
%[text] In this section, the non-inverting amplifier circuit will be analyzed. The op amp will be treated as an ideal op amp.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![noninverting.png](text:image:2ad4)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Non-inverting amplifier*
%[text] Assume that the circuit is functioning within the supply limits,$V\_{\\text{s}\\pm}${"altText":"V\_{\text{s}\pm}"}, and the saturation behavior can be ignored. Under this assumption, the amplifier can be modeled using
%[text]{"align":"center"} $V\_{\\text{out}} = A\_\\text{O} \\cdot (V\_+ - V\_-)${"altText":"V\_{\text{out}} = A\_\text{O} \cdot (V\_+ - V\_-)"}
%[text] Applying the voltage divider law on the feedback circuit:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $V\_{-} = \\frac{R\_1}{R\_2 + R\_1}{V\_{\\text{out}}}${"altText":"V\_{-} = \frac{R\_1}{R\_2 + R\_1}{V\_{\text{out}}}"}
%[text] Substituting the expression into the op amp equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $V\_{\\text{out}} = A\_\\text{O} \\cdot \\left( V\_+ -  \\frac{R\_1}{R\_2 + R\_1}{V\_{\\text{out}}} \\right)${"altText":"V\_{\text{out}} = A\_\text{O} \cdot \left( V\_+ -  \frac{R\_1}{R\_2 + R\_1}{V\_{\text{out}}} \right)"}
%[text] Notice that the feedback loop has caused $V\_{\\text{out}}${"altText":"V\_{\text{out}}"} to appear on both sides of the equation. Now, dividing $A\_\\text{O}${"altText":"A\_\text{O}"} from both sides:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{V\_{\\text{out}}}{A\_\\text{O}} = V\_+ -  \\frac{R\_1}{R\_2 + R\_1}{V\_{\\text{out}}}${"altText":"\frac{V\_{\text{out}}}{A\_\text{O}} = V\_+ -  \frac{R\_1}{R\_2 + R\_1}{V\_{\text{out}}}"}
%[text] Solving for $V\_{\\text{out}}${"altText":"V\_{\text{out}}"} in this equation is certainly possible. However, the term involving $A\_\\text{O}${"altText":"A\_\text{O}"} will be negligible because $A\_\\text{O}${"altText":"A\_\text{O}"} is on the order of $10^5${"altText":"10^5"}. The simple idealized approach is to assume $A\_\\text{O}${"altText":"A\_\text{O}"} is infinite, in which case $V\_{\\text{out}}/A\_\\text{O} \\rightarrow 0${"altText":"V\_{\text{out}}\/A\_\text{O} \rightarrow 0"}. With this assumption:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $0 = V\_+ -  \\frac{R\_1}{R\_2 + R\_1}{V\_{\\text{out}}}${"altText":"0 = V\_+ -  \frac{R\_1}{R\_2 + R\_1}{V\_{\text{out}}}"}
%[text] Implying that the output $V\_{\\text{out}}${"altText":"V\_{\text{out}}"} is related to the input $V\_+${"altText":"V\_+"} as
%[text]{"align":"center"} ${V\_{\\text{out}}} =  \\left( \\frac{R\_2}{R\_1} + 1 \\right) \\cdot V\_+${"altText":"{V\_{\text{out}}} =  \left( \frac{R\_2}{R\_1} + 1 \right) \cdot V\_+"}
%[text] The gain of the non-inverting amplifier is therefore $R\_2/R\_1 + 1${"altText":"R\_2\/R\_1 + 1"}.
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:7a0a) **Activity.** What value of the resistor $R\_2${"altText":"R\_2"} will produce a gain of 10 in a non-inverting amplifier with $R\_1 = 1${"altText":"R\_1 = 1"} k$\\Omega${"altText":"\Omega"}? Verify your answer using the Simscape model:
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![](text:image:11a1)](<matlab: OpAmpNoninverting>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [`OpAmpNoninverting.slx`](<matlab: OpAmpNoninverting>)
%[text] and answer the additional questions listed in the model canvas to complete the activity.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_600047EE] ## Standard op amp circuits
%[text] There are several standard negative feedback op amp circuits that are building blocks of analog circuits. You can explore them in the activity below.
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:973d) **Activity.** In this activity, you will simulate and analyze several negative feedback circuits. During the analysis, assume that the op amp is ideal. This implies you can model the behavior of the op amp with:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $0 = V\_+ - V\_-${"altText":"0 = V\_+ - V\_-"}
%[text] This equation is formed by applying the assumption $A\_\\text{O} = \\infty${"altText":"A\_\text{O} = \infty"} to the equation $V\_{\\text{out}} = A\_\\text{O}\\cdot (V\_+ - V\_-)${"altText":"V\_{\text{out}} = A\_\text{O}\cdot (V\_+ - V\_-)"}.
